// B22, and it is the worst of the five because it is not a crash — it is a contract that
// lies. This was ACCEPTED from v0.0.209, and `burxt explain memory` reported `fill() nothing`.
//
// `push` allocates, into storage the caller owns. The claim was never violated by anything the
// checker looked at, because `push` never asked `has_region()` and `has_region` is the sole
// recorder that credits the allocation to the function containing it. The direct form —
// `let mutable xs: [Int] = []; push(xs, n);` — was caught, but only because the `let` asks.
//
// M14's acceptance item 6 named three paths and had a fail fixture for each: direct, through a
// call, through a `dynamic`. This is a fourth, and three fixtures covering three paths read as
// coverage. A fixture set cannot tell "refuses everything it should" from "refuses everything
// anyone thought to write."
function fill(mutable dst: [Int], n: Int) -> Int allocates nothing {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        push(dst, i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return n;
}
let mutable target: [Int] = [];
print(fill(target, 3));
